pages=[]
pages.append('''<h1>Personalized PageRank on MovieLens 100K:<br>Accuracy, Popularity and Baseline Limitations</h1>
<p class="meta"><b>Elizaveta Gill</b> · Individual · eagill@edu.hse.ru · 2026-10-22<br>LLM4Rec, HSE University · A06 / Week6<br>Assignment: Graph-Based Recommendations with Personalized PageRank</p>
<h2>Abstract</h2><p id="abstract">Recommending unseen movies from sparse explicit ratings requires separating useful personalization from popularity and evaluation artifacts. I implement batched Personalized PageRank in TensorFlow.js on the positive-training MovieLens100K graph and compare it with popularity, global PageRank and three saved matrix-factorization checkpoints under a shared chronological protocol. Validation-selected α=.50 achieves test macro Recall@10 of0.11219 across861 users, compared with0.07602 for popularity, while covering8.57% of the training catalogue. I find support for this PPR implementation as the strongest tested method, but collapsed MF checkpoints and a warm-user offline evaluation prevent general claims about trained MF or deployment benefit.</p>
<p class="small"><b>Index Terms—</b>personalized PageRank, recommender systems, MovieLens, chronological evaluation, reproducibility.</p>
<h2>1. Introduction</h2><p>I rank movies a user has not rated in training or validation, aiming to recover eligible held-out ratings≥4. Missing ratings are unobserved feedback, not dislikes. Useful recommendations should recover relevant movies without merely reproducing popularity or deterministic ties.</p>
<p>For user1, input includes217 training ratings and131 positive graph edges, including <i>Star Wars</i> (ID50); at α=.50 the output begins with <i>Fargo</i> (ID100, score0.000685519, popularity377), an eligible test hit. I contribute a verified batched graph implementation and a controlled comparison that preserves failed baselines rather than interpreting numerical noise as learning. These results establish offline retrieval behavior on the specified cohort, not current intent or business impact.</p>
<h2>2. Related Work and Analysis</h2><p>I use MovieLens with its explicit-feedback limitations [1]. The inspected1998 PageRank manuscript [2] motivates restart-based ranking; topic-sensitive [3] and scalable personalized search [4] motivate different restart distributions and computation reuse. Random-walk collaborative recommendation [5] connects graph proximity to recommendation, while MF [6] is a meaningful alternative only when its fitted factors learn useful structure. TensorFlow.js [7] provides the browser computation substrate; I use its versioned API [8].</p>
<h3>2.1 Data, graph and shared population</h3><p>The same raw files and split as HW5 contain943 users,1,682 movies and100,000 ratings. Unpadded sparsity is1−100000/(943×1682)=93.6953%. I sort each user's rows by timestamp, then movie ID; floor80%/floor10%/remainder yields79,619/9,596/10,785 train/validation/test rows. No user falls below the five-rating exclusion threshold. Time is nondecreasing within users, not globally consistent; these are rating times, not viewing sessions.</p>
<p>All training-observed943 users and1,611 movies form2,554 nodes, with45,602 undirected binary positive edges. User685 and253 movies have degree zero. The catalogue retains these movies; no padding node is added. Validation excludes all training-rated movies; test excludes all training- or validation-rated movies, regardless of rating. Relevance also requires membership in the training pool.</p>'''+cohort+'''<p class="small">The inclusive no-relevance totals97/82 already contain user685; the table shows disjoint exclusions. MF scorability adds none. Positive items outside the pool are4 validation instances/4 distinct movies and13 test instances/13 distinct movies; combined17 instances refer to16 distinct movies. Their user effects are already included above.</p><p class="small">User1/2 have217/49 training ratings and131/32 positive movies. Drama accounts for61/131 and18/32 positives; recommendations contain9/10 and6/10 drama movies. Positive-history mean popularity106.98/132.78 rises to228.4/226.7 in PPR. At the primary setting, user-list overlap is1/10 for popularity, PageRank and PPR, and2/10 for MF; different exclusions also affect these counts.</p>''')
pages.append('''<div class="running">A06 · Elizaveta Gill</div><h2>2. Related Work and Analysis (continued)</h2><p>I consider popularity as the cheap baseline, MF as a learned alternative requiring a credible fit, and PPR as a graph-based candidate for replacing the tested collapsed baseline. For an undirected component, π<sub>i</sub>=d<sub>i</sub>/(2|E|) is stationary because π<sub>i</sub>Q<sub>ij</sub>=A<sub>ij</sub>/(2|E|). Finite restart changes this distribution; the bipartite walk can oscillate without restart. The measured PageRank–popularity Spearman correlations are.98770/.99267/.99665/.99870 at α=.50/.70/.85/.95; mean test Top10 intersections are6.931/7.647/8.305/9.289 items, over861 users.</p>
<p>A dense square float32 transition would hold26.09MB versus6.08MB for rectangular R (payload estimates, decimal MB). The measured two-matmul median with a scalar barrier is5.3ms for one column and14.7ms for943 columns; these are kernel proxies, not full propagation times. Full measured global/PPR runs appear in §4.3. Single-user mode is retained for verification; full batching amortizes launches and keeps the numerical rule unchanged.</p>
<h2>3. Method</h2><p>My pipeline is split → graph → normalization → propagation → movie extraction → exclusion → score-descending/ID-ascending ranking → macro recall. Node order is ascending raw user IDs, then ascending raw movie IDs. On MovieLens the adapter accepts a <b>raw user ID</b> and maps it; an unknown ID throws. Explicit transition fixtures instead accept a bounds-checked node index.</p>
<div class="equation">p<sub>0</sub>=v;&nbsp; p<sub>t+1</sub>=α(Qᵀp<sub>t</sub>+d<sub>t</sub>v)+(1−α)v,&nbsp; d<sub>t</sub>=Σ<sub>i:degree(i)=0</sub>p<sub>t,i</sub>.</div>
<p>Q has normalized nonzero rows and zero dangling rows; I preserve its required plain-array return and mask. Global restart is uniform, while PPR restart is one-hot at the user. Dangling mass returns through the same v; I do not renormalize to conceal mass errors. MovieLens propagation uses R·(p<sub>M</sub>/d<sub>M</sub>) and Rᵀ·(p<sub>U</sub>/d<sub>U</sub>) with <code>tf.matMul</code>, never a dense square GPU transition.</p>
<p>The943 seed columns stop independently at L1 residual&lt;10⁻⁶ or500 iterations, freezing each converged column. Per-iteration synchronous readback is only943 float32 residuals (3,772B); synchronous callers require <code>dataSync()</code>, which may block the UI. Final Float64Array storage does not recover precision lost in float32 GPU arithmetic. Explicit disposal and synchronous <code>tf.tidy()</code> scopes manage tensors.</p>
<p>A graph-identity cache keys method/seed mode, α, tolerance, cap, batch size and backend; result copies prevent mutation. Source-content changes require explicit cache invalidation. The recorded runtime is TensorFlow.js4.22.0, WebGL on Apple M3, Chrome153.0.8010.12; independent analysis uses NumPy2.3.5 float64. I set the slider to the validation winner before the separate test evaluation, avoiding the combined runner's test-before-selection order. Exact validation ties select smaller α; global PageRank uses the same α without separate optimization. MF selects earliest strictly improving validation RMSE, a different objective.</p>
<h2>4. Experiments and Verification</h2><h3>4.1 Validation selection and primary test comparison</h3>'''+sweep+'''<p class="small">846 validation users select α=.50. Test uses861 shared users. Mean popularity is mean positive training degree across each list, then users; coverage is distinct recommended movies/1,611. Light degree≤33 includes435 users; heavy degree&gt;33 includes426.</p>'''+tab(['Method','Recall@10','Mean pop.','Distinct / coverage','Light recall','Heavy recall'],primaryrows)+'''<p class="small"><b>MF provenance:</b> HW6 supplied reference:16 factors,30 epochs, batch512, Adam lr=.01, L2=.05, seed20261013, checkpoint1. HW5 <code>default-20261016-01</code> and <code>reg-0005-20261016-01</code>:16 factors,20 epochs, batch64, lr=.01, L2=.05/.005, init seeds5101/5102 and shuffle5103, checkpoint1 each. I retain both separately; no test-based winner is invented. The supplied reference is the primary baseline; both previous MF models satisfy the assignment's continuity comparison. Its raw held-out RMSE population differs from HW5's warm-only RMSE population.</p>''')
pages.append('''<div class="running">A06 · Elizaveta Gill</div><h2>4. Experiments and Verification (continued)</h2><h3>4.2 Actual Top10 lists: primary α=.50, test exclusions</h3><p class="small">All three MF checkpoints have identical eligible rankings; one MF column represents them. Every MF score is3.580603 (rounded); PPR scores below are probabilities multiplied by10⁴ for readability, not ratings. “Pop” counts positive training users; Y marks an eligible test hit. Full titles, genres and unscaled scores are in <a href="../week6/report/recommendations_supplement.html">the local supplement</a>.</p>
<h3>User1: PPR mean popularity228.4; MF72.2</h3>'''+usertable(1)+'''<p class="small">PPR hit IDs{100,222,258}: Recall@10=3/19=.157895. MF retrieves7/19=.368421 for this user despite its constant scores; PPR is not better for every individual.</p>
<h3>User2: PPR mean popularity226.7; MF111.4</h3>'''+usertable(2)+'''<p class="small">Both retrieve0/2=0: eligible relevant movies are <i>Fly Away Home</i> (304) and <i>As Good As It Gets</i> (316). This is a concrete failure case, not evidence that those unseen movies are disliked.</p>
<p class="small"><b>Genre key:</b> Ac action; Ad adventure; An animation; Ch children's; Co comedy; Cr crime; Do documentary; Dr drama; Fa fantasy; Ro romance; SF science fiction; Th thriller; Wa war. Genres are multilabel. The long alternative title of <i>Shanghai Triad</i> is shortened only in this table.</p>
''')
pages.append('''<div class="running">A06 · Elizaveta Gill</div><h2>4. Experiments and Verification (continued)</h2><h3>4.3 Numerical, runtime and memory evidence</h3>'''+runtime+'''<p class="small">Each PPR run covers all943 graph users, not just the861 test cohort; the degree-zero seed converges in one iteration. Timings are measured on the recorded host, include propagation setup/readback, and are not cross-device benchmarks. Cold compilation affects comparisons. At primary α=.50 all943 converge; maximum residual9.9944×10⁻⁷. At.95,29/943 float32 columns reach the cap; float64 converges for943/943, with identical filtered Top10 lists. Complete rankings can differ at numerical near-ties; I do not claim bitwise equality.</p>'''+checks+'''<h3>Worked reference-graph update</h3><p class="small">For A→B,C; B→C; C→A; D→C, there are no dangling nodes. With uniform p₀=v=(¼,¼,¼,¼), incoming mass is Qᵀp₀=(¼,⅛,⅝,0): A receives¼ from C; B receives⅛ from A; C receives⅛+¼+¼. At α=.85:</p>
<div class="equation">p₁=.85(¼,⅛,⅝,0)+.15(¼,¼,¼,¼)<br>=(.25,.14375,.56875,.0375).</div>
<p class="small">Restart only at A gives p₁=.85(0,½,½,0)+.15(1,0,0,0)=(.15,.425,.425,0). The browser stationary vectors differ from the official uniform/seeded references by at most1.53×10⁻⁷/1.14×10⁻⁷, preserving C&gt;A&gt;B&gt;D and A&gt;C&gt;B&gt;D. This is an agent-executed worked cross-check, not a claim of unaided personal calculation.</p>
<div class="shot"><img src="assets/local-evaluation.png" alt="Actual local method comparison after evaluation"></div><p class="caption">Figure1. <b>Local</b> comparison panel after evaluation at α=.50; cropped presentation of the saved browser screenshot, not a recreated table. Its inclusive82 no-relevance exclusions already contain user685. Source: <a href="../week6/report/assets/local-evaluation.png">original screenshot</a>.</p>''')
pages.append('''<div class="running">A06 · Elizaveta Gill</div><h2>5. Discussion</h2><h3>5.1 What supports PPR, and what fails</h3><p>I favor replacing the <i>tested collapsed MF baseline</i> with PPR as the candidate for a controlled pilot, not immediate rollout. PPR improves macro recall over popularity by3.62 percentage points (+47.6% relative) and covers138/1,611 movies versus82/1,611. Yet its mean overlap with popularity is6.393/10 (PageRank5.612/10; each MF.899/10), so personalization retains strong popularity influence. Lower popularity does not establish novelty or discovery value.</p>
<p>The PPR gain over popularity is larger among light users: +5.67 percentage points (+75.2% relative), versus+1.52 points (+19.8%) for heavy users. These descriptive groups differ in activity and relevance denominators; I claim neither causality nor statistical significance. User2's0/2 failure and user1's stronger MF recall caution against equating aggregate superiority with universal benefit.</p>
<h3>5.2 Controlled diagnostics and exclusions</h3>'''+diag+'''<p>User switching changes both restart and exclusions: user1 has1,367 eligible test candidates; user2 has1,556. Six of user1's baseline movies are excluded for user2, and all ten of user2's are excluded for user1. Restricting both α=.85 rankings to their common1,324 candidates gives8/10 overlap rather than0/10. Thus the original “seed-only” display does not isolate personalization. For fixed user1, changing α=.85→.50 retains7/10 items, lowers popularity by17.1 (245.5→228.4) and keeps3/19 hits; this is sensitivity, not a general accuracy improvement.</p>
<h3>5.3 MF collapse and the next experiment</h3><p>The HW6 fit selected epoch1; all30 validation RMSEs tie. Mean user/item factor-row norms are3.527×10⁻⁵/3.516×10⁻⁵, and every audited browser score is constant, yielding ascending-ID ranking. Independent test RMSE1.228900137185 matches the mean-only1.228900137184 to numerical precision. Identical HW5 rankings were separately verified. These three collapsed checkpoints cannot establish PPR's superiority to successfully trained MF.</p>
<p>The loss is batch-mean MSE plus λ(||U||²+||V||²) over the whole matrices on every batch (<code>week6/script.js:949–954</code>). Repeated shrinkage is a plausible mechanism; tiny dot products disappear when added to the mean in float32. This code inspection is not a controlled causal diagnosis. I did not repair or retrain the model at this stage, so no successful fix is claimed.</p>
<p>Exploratory validation Recall@10 is.12400 at α=.10 and.12136 at.30, both over846 users; neither was evaluated on test, and primary α=.50 remains fixed. My next experiment would validate a genuinely learning MF baseline and predeclare any extended PPR validation grid before a fresh test. The current split is not globally temporal, excludes cold cases and uses explicit ratings rather than exposure or viewing outcomes.</p>
<p><b>Proposed online test, not completed:</b> after repairing MF, randomize users persistently across devices to PPR versus validated MF shelves. Predeclare per-user recommendation click-through rate as primary; guard latency, empty-list rate and retention. Hold other shelves and eligibility fixed. Offline recall cannot establish online effects.</p>
<h2>6. AI Usage Disclosure</h2><p class="small">I used Codex for implementation, execution, independent verification, source checks and report preparation. I specified preservation boundaries, MF provenance, raw-ID seeds and validation-before-test selection. Native metadata records <code>gpt-6-astra</code> and CLI <code>0.163.0-alpha.10.2</code>; the desktop build was not recorded. Historical experiment evidence records TensorFlow.js4.22.0, Chrome153.0.8010.12 and NumPy2.3.5. Playwright1.62.1 identifies the current report renderer, not an inferred historical harness.</p>
<p class="small"><b>Session notes:</b> <code>gill_elizaveta_a06_session.json</code> contains1,804 records from four native JSONL parts. They were combined in their original order into one JSON array by adding brackets and separators. Every record retains its serialized content: there were zero replacements, redactions or omitted records. The format changed, so this is <b>not a byte-identical native export</b>. The prepared JSON and gzip were copied unchanged; decompression reproduces the JSON byte-for-byte.</p>
<p class="small">The16,443,699-byte JSON spans22 October2026,09:39:25.371–11:44:39.505 UTC. Later packaging actions are outside this snapshot; elapsed conversation time is not active working time. Original multipart files and earlier sanitized intermediates are retained only as local backups outside the submission folder. Session exports and private logs are excluded from the public repository. The over10MB delivery procedure still requires instructor confirmation. Tests, calculations and PDF checks were agent-executed, not unaided personal verification. My personal review remains pending; I do not claim first-hand reading of every paper.</p>''')
